all right now let's get labor's take on  things this morning from shadow home  secretary yvette cooper good morning  shadow home secretary  good morning trevor  now last week on this program  your colleague jonathan reynolds said  that sanctions on russia have had a  significant effect um  oddly enough the ruble is now worth more  than it was before the invasion  do you think that we need to do more  than is currently being  proposed by government we just heard  from kit malthouse  we do support stronger action we support  the action that the government has taken  to provide military support for ukraine  to be able to defend itself and to  continue to do so and we also support  strong sanctions and we think we need to  be continually looking at where further  we can go to put maximum economic  pressure  on russia and on putin's government that  includes looking at individual sanctions  that also includes  looking more widely but we also need to  do this in a united way across not just  europe but across the world because i  think it's been that united front that  has been really important in  addressing this  really cruel and vile illegal war from  putin  are you worried that the united front is  essentially being punctured a bit by the  german reluctance to  stop gas we understand how difficult it  is for them but  it's not as difficult as it is for the  ukrainian people is it  obviously it's hugely difficult for the  ukrainian people and they have shown  incredible bravery in the response uh to  this illegal invasion as well and also  in response to war crimes that are  taking place and the terrible  bombardment so that's why we do yes urge  germany and other countries to really  all work together to try and reduce that  dependency on  russian oil and gas and to actually have  alternatives because this is obviously a  part of a broader sanctions approach we  have to have the strongest possible  action to put the pressure on  there are things that we can do  ourselves and i want to come to the  issue of refugees in a moment which is  your uh  portfolio but on the question of weapons  um prime minister's offering  more a fresh delivery of weapons to the  to ukraine  yesterday  um do you think that we are doing enough  on the military and uh the weapons end  of things  well we support the action the  government's taken on this and i think  this is an important area as you say  there are huge differences between us  and the government on the support for  refugees where we don't think the  government's gone far enough but we  actually support the government on the  action that they've taken to provide  military support for ukraine and it's  good i think that we've got that cross  party agreement on that and we're going  to have to continue to do so because  this doesn't look like this is coming to  a quick end uh it's important that some  of russia's objectives have been  frustrated by the bravery of what  ukraine has been doing but we also know  that they are continuing this really  horrendous bombardment and we've seen  obviously the cases of torture as well  but i think the issue around the  refugees visas is really important it is  shocking it is just truly shameful the  long delays that we have in getting  ukrainian families visas to come stay  with british families who want to host  and support them 30 000 people are  waiting for visas around half of them  have been waiting for more than 10 days  many of them have been waiting for two  for three weeks now  and having filled in all of the forms  this is just disgraceful delays and i do  not understand why prissy patel is so  failing to get a grip on this  well uh whatever they're doing one  simple way of cutting through this would  simply be to drop the visa requirement  as has been the case in uh countries on  the continent would labor support the  removal of the visa requirement  for ukrainian uh asylum seekers  essentially  we think you should drop the extra  bureaucracy you can simply do  suggestions i'm asking specifically  about the visa requirement  yeah  so  so we described a kind of emergency visa  which is effectively just the security  checks that's what we need to do you  don't need a lot of this additional visa  requirements for example to come on the  homes for ukraine scheme ukrainian  families are supposed to prove their  residence as to where they were living  before the 1st of january so people are  uploading utility bills or other kinds  of details that then has to be checked  by caseworkers that then adds to delays  the point about security checks is  ministers and officials have themselves  admitted you can do them on the spot you  can do them within a matter of hours so  there is absolutely no excuse for these  weeks of delays we should be able to  just do this and grant people permission  to come pretty much instantly and get  those checks done straight away  so to be clear  an emergency visa is still a visa but  you  would only apply  one test  which you think could be passed within  hours about whether somebody's a  security risk or not  do you really think believe that yes  how would you know with the minister how  would you know somebody says  i'm i'm not a russian i'm not a member  of the wagner group i'm not coming to us  how would we actually be able to know  that within hours  these are the standard security checks  that we already do um sort of standard  checks with with passports and so on and  ministers and officials have themselves  said they've themselves said that  security checks can be done online  they've themselves said these security  checks can be done either on the spot  you're effectively checking somebody  against a database of wanted criminals  you're you're checking people in that  respect so those are the kinds of checks  that you can do really quickly and  ministers and officials have both  admitted that they can do them really  quickly that's not what's holding things  up it's not the security checks it's the  fact that they've added huge amounts of  bureaucracy that they've never got a  grip of this and bear in mind the home  office had the opportunity to prepare  they had months to prepare for this it's  six weeks ago we were urging them to  take action and they haven't done so  pretty patel wanted to simply use the  existing visa system you remember we had  that awful moment when a home office  minister effectively was saying people  should get a seasonal workers visa  perhaps to come and pick fruit they  tried not to set up a proper scheme and  then when they did do so they added huge  amounts of bureaucracy to it but it's  letting everyone down you have disabled  disabled man for example who can't leave  ukraine until he's got his full visa in  a case that i know of you have moms and  kids who are running out of money and it  is shameful british people want to be  generous and want to provide support and  pretty patel is not letting them  okay forgive me for for hiring you up  but uh we do need to talk about domestic  politics a bit one question uh or maybe  a couple questions on on this um  your  former shadow chancellor john mcdonnell  said in 2019 uh in the manifesto  actually that you fought on that labour  would scrap non-dom status altogether uh  is the future labor government still  going to do that  well rachel's rachel reeves has set up a  review of all  those sorts of tax exemptions and tax  arrangements and that is underway at the  moment but look there is a clear issue  at the moment which is why you're asking  the question about rishi sunak's  household tax arrangements at a time  when he has been putting taxes up for  everyone asking everybody else to pay  over a thousand pounds more  his household has had special tax  arrangements that allow them to pay less  tax that is just unfair and that i think  is why people are so angry about what's  been happening and also the fact that  they've just not been transparent about  it when there could have been conflicts  of interest in place as well well we all  but um what everybody acknowledges is  that what she has done is perfectly  lawful uh as far as i understand it lots  of other people do something similar and  you might think that you know it's  ethical to do something different but  that she hasn't actually done anything  wrong and i'm wondering whether labour  has the view  that this  perfectly lawful status should now not  be available to people  well as i said rachel has a review  underway and we have previously proposed  reforms in exactly this area but the  issue now is that actually the ethics  matter if you are part of a household  where you are asking people across the  country to pay more to put taxes up to a  70-year high and everybody across the  country is really feeling the strain i  don't know how people are going to be  managing with the cost of living indeed  and  indeed and i asked mr house about that  but the problem the problem is in place  i think that is a serious problem i i  understand the point but it's quite  difficult to take the complaint  that she should have done something  different if you can't say that you  would make it unlawful  so i don't think that's right i think  that ethics do matter when you're in  government particularly there may be a  conflict of interest here as well so we  don't know for example whether the  chancellor declared a conflict of  interest when he was making decisions on  policies there's some policies reported  that do affect  non-doms people with non-dom status we  also don't know  what the facts are around some of the  reports about whether they've been  beneficiaries from tax havens and we  also don't know  some of the wider issues about why the  chancellor has had a permanent residence  card in the u.s at the same time as  indeed parliament and the member of  indeed and i asked mr mulhouse about  that i'm interrupting you simply because  in order to make sure that you don't  have a conflict of interest sorry i'm  trevor sorry  forgive me i'm interrupting you because  i know that you want to talk about  antisocial behavior and i just wanted to  give you half a minute to to say uh  labor's got a policy out uh today that  to tackle  antisocial behavior by creating  police hubs and so on why not just more  police  we do think that we need more police and  particularly more police out on the beat  in our communities we had huge cuts to  policing which the government has  finally accepted it needs to start to  reverse although there are still fewer  police than there were in 2010 but even  now we're still not seeing the police  back in the neighborhoods it was one of  the most important policies labor had  was to set up neighborhood policing in  our communities to tackle anti-social  behavior we're at a time when crime has  gone up when prosecutions have gone down  and in many communities anti-social  behavior is rife and isn't being dealt  with that's why we want to set up  neighborhood police hubs in communities  in towns right across the country to  bring together the police local council  officers to tackle that anti-social  behavior that is making people's lives a  misery and we pay for it by saying  cancel the royal yacht that neither the  navy nor the royal family want and use  that money to cut crime and anti-social  behavior well i'll give you many more  cops after that uh counseling the royal  yacht and and that's 200 million in one  year it has  i've done some arithmetic it gives you  about  i think 60 police officers  in each police force 43 issues of 43  police forces across england  so the royal yacht funding would fund  the hubs it would fund the centers for  example in castleford in my constituency  we now have we've persuaded the police  and the council to work together to set  up a hub there's another one redbridge  council have been setting them up which  are basically a base for local police  and councils to be officers to be based  in the community rather than to be based  miles away to be based locally in the  community and to work with the community  in hot spots of anti-social behavior and  to set up neighborhood prevention teams  what the conservatives have done is they  just collapsed any of the work on  anti-social behavior we used to have  loads of things going on they pulled  back from it they completely decimated  neighborhood policing we're saying bring  back neighborhood policing and bring  back the action on anti-social behaviour  as well  yvette cooper thank you very much for  your time this morning  thank you

